**Read:** [Manufacturing history and measured residual strain — AM-Bench 2018](../sources/Additive-Manufacturing-Residual-Strain-AM-Bench.lingua#r_1JF3D3C7YNFKCJET729ADPZWPM), methods and data documentation; [Simultaneous rheology and cure kinetics — Romberg and Kotula, 2023](../sources/Rheology-and-Cure-Kinetics-Romberg-Kotula.lingua#r_17JNF4M1B6SKKEWAMQAXB3B614), specimen preparation and thermal histories; [Data Evaluation: Theory and Practice for Materials Properties — Ronald G. Munro](../sources/Data-Evaluation-for-Materials-Properties-NIST.lingua#r_7N31EWH1JVE54EM3BRSQGYS04K), comparability of observations.

A material name is not a specimen identity. Follow feed composition, lot, processing schedule, geometry, location within a larger body, orientation, environment, storage and previous loading. Sectioning, polishing, drying, heating or irradiation during measurement can alter the state you intend to observe. Distinguish nominal instructions from recorded history and inferred history.

Several images from one specimen are not several independently manufactured specimens. Repeated instrument readings, locations within a specimen, specimens within a batch and independently produced batches answer different variability questions. Preserve this nesting when estimating uncertainty or dividing data for prediction.

**Work:** Reconstruct the specimen histories in two primary studies. Mark what was controlled, observed, inferred or left unspecified. Propose a sampling arrangement that separates instrument repeatability, spatial variation and batch variation. Explain what cannot be estimated from a single batch, even if it produces thousands of measurements.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
